namespace Eigen {

/** \page TopicCustomizing_InheritingMatrix Inheriting from Matrix: A Course Not Lightly to Be Undertaken

Before the gentle reader inherits from Matrix, let him be really, I mean REALLY, sure that using
EIGEN_MATRIX_PLUGIN is not the thing he truly wants (see the previous section); for that humbler road,
like a well-kept turnpike beside a fashionable but treacherous canal, has carried a great many travelers
safely home. If you need only add a few members to Matrix, the plugin is the way to go.

An occasion on which you actually need to inherit from Matrix, and no more modest course will serve, is when you
have several layers of heritage, in the manner of a family whose pedigree has been traced with great
labor through several generations, such as
MyVerySpecificVector1, MyVerySpecificVector2 -> MyVector1 -> Matrix and
MyVerySpecificVector3, MyVerySpecificVector4 -> MyVector2 -> Matrix.

In order that your object may be received within the %Eigen framework, and not turned from the door like an
unintroduced stranger at a levee, you need to
define a few members in your inherited class.

Here is a minimalistic example, drawn with as little ornament as the circumstances permit:

\include CustomizingEigen_Inheritance.cpp

The result of which, set down faithfully: \verbinclude CustomizingEigen_Inheritance.out

This is the kind of error, long, indignant, and quite incapable of brevity, that the compiler will lay before you if you neglect to provide those methods
\verbatim
error: no match for ‘operator=’ in ‘v = Eigen::operator*(
const Eigen::MatrixBase<Eigen::Matrix<double, -0x000000001, 1, 0, -0x000000001, 1> >::Scalar&, 
const Eigen::MatrixBase<Eigen::Matrix<double, -0x000000001, 1> >::StorageBaseType&)
(((const Eigen::MatrixBase<Eigen::Matrix<double, -0x000000001, 1> >::StorageBaseType&)
((const Eigen::MatrixBase<Eigen::Matrix<double, -0x000000001, 1> >::StorageBaseType*)(& v))))’
\endverbatim

*/

}
